WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.527
Welcome to Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction to Quantum

00:00:03.527 --> 00:00:07.837
Computing, a twelve-lesson Codex 67 series built around real code,

00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:11.364
measured outputs, and one verified concept at a time.

00:00:11.364 --> 00:00:16.066
In Lesson One, we move from classical bits to measured qubits by

00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:20.376
comparing three states: a definite zero, a definite one, and an

00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:21.160
equal superposition.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.733
The goal is to predict each result, run every circuit for one

00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:29.577
thousand shots in a local Qiskit simulator, and then verify the

00:00:29.577 --> 00:00:32.722
difference between certainty and probability with both raw counts

00:00:32.722 --> 00:00:34.119
and a visual comparison.

00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:38.313
A classical bit is always read as either zero or one.

00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:42.820
A qubit can also be measured as zero or one, but before measurement

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.594
its state can be prepared with quantum gates.

00:00:45.594 --> 00:00:48.714
The measurement statistics tell us how that preparation behaves.

00:00:49.094 --> 00:00:51.421
One measurement gives one classical answer.

00:00:51.421 --> 00:00:55.687
To learn the distribution, we repeat the same circuit many times.

00:00:55.687 --> 00:00:58.014
Each repetition is called a shot.

00:00:58.014 --> 00:01:01.116
Here, every experiment uses exactly one thousand shots.

00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:05.442
The demonstration is a small Python file opened in Visual Studio

00:01:05.442 --> 00:01:09.388
Code. It uses Qiskit Quantum Circuit and the Statevector Sampler, so

00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:13.334
the entire lesson runs locally without sending a job to quantum

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:13.693
hardware.

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:18.571
Near the top of the file, shots is set to one thousand and the

00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:20.820
simulator seed is set to sixty-seven.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.355
The fixed seed makes this teaching run reproducible, while the shot

00:01:24.355 --> 00:01:26.604
count keeps all three experiments directly comparable.

00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:29.282
The helper function creates a one-qubit circuit.

00:01:29.282 --> 00:01:33.878
It adds an X gate only when the gate name is X, adds a Hadamard gate

00:01:33.878 --> 00:01:37.037
only when the name is H, and then measures the qubit.

00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:40.643
The experiment list calls that same helper three times.

00:01:40.643 --> 00:01:45.302
The identity label adds no gate, X prepares the one state, and H

00:01:45.302 --> 00:01:46.735
prepares an equal superposition.

00:01:46.735 --> 00:01:50.319
This keeps the circuit structure consistent while changing only the

00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:51.036
preparation step.

00:01:51.416 --> 00:01:56.308
First is the baseline. A newly created qubit starts in ket zero, and

00:01:56.308 --> 00:01:58.566
no gate changes it before measurement.

00:01:58.566 --> 00:02:01.953
My prediction is complete certainty: all one thousand measurements

00:02:01.953 --> 00:02:03.082
should return zero.

00:02:03.462 --> 00:02:07.528
Second, the X gate flips the computational basis state from zero to

00:02:07.528 --> 00:02:11.255
one. My prediction is again complete certainty, but in the opposite

00:02:11.255 --> 00:02:13.965
direction: all one thousand measurements should return one.

00:02:14.345 --> 00:02:18.452
Third, the Hadamard gate transforms the starting zero state into an

00:02:18.452 --> 00:02:20.692
equal superposition of zero and one.

00:02:20.692 --> 00:02:24.799
I predict a distribution close to fifty-fifty, not a guaranteed

00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:27.039
exact split in every finite sample.

00:02:27.419 --> 00:02:30.285
The Statevector Sampler receives all three measured circuits

00:02:30.285 --> 00:02:34.583
together. It executes each one for the same one thousand shots, and

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:38.880
the program collects the resulting counts in the same order as the

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.597
experiment list.

00:02:39.977 --> 00:02:43.736
Now I will run the prepared lesson from the integrated terminal at

00:02:43.736 --> 00:02:45.615
the bottom of Visual Studio Code.

00:02:45.615 --> 00:02:48.748
The runner adds clear result headings while preserving the original

00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:50.314
circuit diagrams and measurement counts.

00:02:50.694 --> 00:02:54.687
Result one is definite zero. The circuit contains only measurement,

00:02:54.687 --> 00:02:58.679
and the terminal reports one thousand zeros with no ones.

00:02:58.679 --> 00:03:01.075
That exactly matches the baseline prediction.

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:05.908
Result two is definite one. The circuit diagram shows the X gate

00:03:05.908 --> 00:03:09.620
before measurement, and the terminal reports one thousand ones with

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.589
no zeros. That exactly matches the second prediction.

00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:14.712
Result three is equal superposition.

00:03:14.712 --> 00:03:18.197
The Hadamard circuit returns five hundred seven zeros and four

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:22.031
hundred ninety-three ones. The total is one thousand, and the

00:03:22.031 --> 00:03:25.517
difference is small enough to show the expected near-even

00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:25.866
distribution.

00:03:26.246 --> 00:03:30.048
The superposition result is not exactly five hundred and five

00:03:30.048 --> 00:03:32.709
hundred because finite sampling includes ordinary statistical

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:36.131
variation. A different unseeded run would usually produce slightly

00:03:36.131 --> 00:03:39.553
different counts while preserving the same overall fifty-fifty

00:03:39.553 --> 00:03:39.933
pattern.

00:03:40.313 --> 00:03:45.254
This does not mean the qubit is measured as half of each value in

00:03:45.254 --> 00:03:48.784
one shot. Every individual shot still returns one classical bit.

00:03:48.784 --> 00:03:51.961
The quantum behavior appears in the distribution collected across

00:03:51.961 --> 00:03:53.372
repeated preparations and measurements.

00:03:53.752 --> 00:03:56.165
The comparison chart makes the distinction visible.

00:03:56.165 --> 00:03:59.268
Definite zero has all of its weight on zero.

00:03:59.268 --> 00:04:02.370
Definite one has all of its weight on one.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.127
Equal superposition divides its measured outcomes almost evenly

00:04:05.127 --> 00:04:06.162
across both values.

00:04:06.542 --> 00:04:10.095
The first two circuits demonstrate deterministic measurement in the

00:04:10.095 --> 00:04:12.858
computational basis. The Hadamard circuit demonstrates probabilistic

00:04:12.858 --> 00:04:15.227
measurement after a different state preparation.

00:04:15.227 --> 00:04:19.965
The code path is nearly identical, but the gate changes the observed

00:04:19.965 --> 00:04:20.360
distribution.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.025
I verify the lesson in three ways.

00:04:23.025 --> 00:04:26.617
The counts total one thousand for every circuit, the definite-state

00:04:26.617 --> 00:04:29.229
outputs exactly match their predictions, and the superposition

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:33.801
output is close to an even split in both the terminal and the chart.

00:04:34.181 --> 00:04:38.841
This is an ideal local simulation, not a claim about a physical

00:04:38.841 --> 00:04:41.559
quantum processor. Real hardware introduces noise, calibration

00:04:41.559 --> 00:04:43.889
limits, queue time, and measurement error.

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:48.161
The simulator is useful here because it isolates the circuit logic.

00:04:48.541 --> 00:04:52.289
The fixed seed, equal shot count, preserved source code, and

00:04:52.289 --> 00:04:54.538
recorded outputs make the lesson reproducible.

00:04:54.538 --> 00:04:57.912
Someone using the same environment can inspect the circuit

00:04:57.912 --> 00:05:01.286
definitions, rerun the command, and compare the same three

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:02.036
measurement patterns.

00:05:02.416 --> 00:05:06.189
To summarize, the approach was to hold the simulator and shot count

00:05:06.189 --> 00:05:08.391
constant while changing only the state preparation.

00:05:08.391 --> 00:05:10.907
The challenge was interpreting the small sampling difference

00:05:10.907 --> 00:05:13.423
correctly instead of treating it as an error.

00:05:13.423 --> 00:05:16.882
The terminal counts and comparison chart confirm the quality of the

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:17.196
result.

00:05:17.576 --> 00:05:22.059
Lesson One gives us three anchor points: certain zero, certain one,

00:05:22.059 --> 00:05:26.541
and equal probability. In Lesson Two, we will replace the fixed

00:05:26.541 --> 00:05:30.208
Hadamard preparation with a parameterized rotation and watch the

00:05:30.208 --> 00:05:32.653
probability change continuously with the angle.

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:36.932
That completes Lesson One of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction

00:05:36.932 --> 00:05:40.831
to Quantum Computing: real code, measured outputs, and one verified

00:05:40.831 --> 00:05:45.511
concept at a time. The runnable Lesson One code is linked below.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:49.410
Follow Renaissance Field Lite for all twelve Codex 67 lessons.
